B: 3664 ns per operation (median)
A needs 55.3% less time than B: B takes 2.24 times as long as A.
With 95% confidence the difference lies between 52.5% and 56.9% less time (B takes between 2.1 and 2.32 times as long as A).
Noise floor: 3.66%. Identical code measured against itself on this setup can look that different, so smaller differences mean nothing.
Neighbouring measurements are correlated (autocorrelation +0.63), so they were resampled in blocks of 3.

Verdict: RESOLVED. A is faster than B: the difference is real, as its interval excludes zero and it exceeds the noise floor.

Warnings:
  - the program holds 218 MB of live data, more than the caches of many machines; for data that size, where it lies in memory can shift the result by several points in ways the interval does not cover, and differently in the next process; run the comparison in several processes with the multiproc package
  - the ratio B/A was 6.85% lower in the second half of the run than in the first, so the candidates had not reached a steady state and the difference depends on how long the run was; a common cause is a head start for whichever candidate ran alone last before the comparison or had its data built last, which a longer CollectOptions.WarmupDuration removes

